# Force LF for all text files regardless of the checking-out machine's OS
# or local `core.autocrlf` setting. Without this, a Windows checkout with
# `core.autocrlf=true` (Git for Windows' own recommended default) silently
# converts every file to CRLF on checkout — which then looks like every
# file in the repo has "changed" the moment anything (an editor save, an
# externally-applied patch/zip, a `git add --renormalize .`) writes LF
# back, even though the actual code content never moved. This repo's
# history and CI are LF throughout; this file makes that the enforced,
# machine-independent default instead of relying on every contributor's
# local git config agreeing by coincidence.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files and any future .cmd/.bat scripts genuinely need
# CRLF to run correctly under cmd.exe — carve out an explicit exception
# rather than let text=auto guess.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
